---
description: Engineering rules for Next.js personal finance app
globs:
  - app/**/*.ts
  - app/**/*.tsx
  - components/**/*.ts
  - components/**/*.tsx
  - lib/**/*.ts
alwaysApply: true
---

Architecture rules:

- Use Next.js App Router
- Prefer Server Components by default
- Use Client Components only for interactivity
- Keep business logic out of UI components
- Keep finance calculations in dedicated domain modules
- Keep AI orchestration isolated from presentation code
- Keep database access isolated behind repository or service functions

Code organization:

- app/ for routes and screens
- components/ for reusable UI
- lib/finance for finance domain logic
- lib/ai for AI agent orchestration
- lib/export for CSV/XLSX generation
- lib/analytics for aggregation logic
- lib/categorization for merchant and category mapping
- lib/validation for schemas
- types/ for domain types

Best practices:

- Use TypeScript strictly
- Validate all inputs with schemas
- Create explicit domain types for transaction, category, budget, holding, goal, recurring payment
- Use deterministic functions for analytics and categorization whenever possible
- Use AI only where interpretation or summarization adds value
- Never use AI for core financial math
- Financial math must be deterministic and testable

Performance:

- Precompute expensive dashboard aggregates
- Cache derived summaries where sensible
- Use Redis for lightweight caching and chat/session support
- Avoid unnecessary client-side fetching
- Stream AI responses where useful
- Paginate heavy transaction lists

Reliability:

- Handle missing or partial banking data gracefully
- Mark uncertain categorizations explicitly
- Make all financial transformations auditable
- Keep raw transaction data immutable where possible
- Log category overrides and rule changes

Security and privacy:

- Treat financial data as highly sensitive
- Minimize data sent to LLMs
- Redact unnecessary identifiers
- Never send full raw financial history to the model when summarization context is enough
- Prefer structured summaries over raw dumps
- Keep secrets server-side only

Developer behavior:

- When creating a new feature, also consider:
  - analytics implications
  - export implications
  - AI explanation implications
  - responsiveness
  - empty states
  - loading states
  - error states
